WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.640
"Early in the history of warfare, man
learned that the best formula for victory

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.450
was compounded in striking power,
mobility,

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:10.160
and self-protection."

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:15.620
The US is easily the most powerful
country to have ever existed.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.930
And it certainly spends more on its
military budget and any other country

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:19.680
does today.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.080
That's all pretty well-known,

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.880
but less well-known is the fact that the
US has around 800 military bases around the globe.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.780
This map can't even show that many dots.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.220
These are just 200 or so dots to give you
an idea of where these bases are clustered.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:41.640
These bases are everything from massive military compounds to small airstrips in the middle of the ocean.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.140
If you combined all the foreign bases that every other nation has outside of its own borders,

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.640
you get a total of about thirty.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.879
There's never been a country with such a

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:53.559
massive global presence.

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:57.210
And the big question is: how did these bases
get there the first place?

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:01.060
And how much is it costing US taxpayers
to keep them open?

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.540
Most to these bases cropped up after
WWII, when the US set up in places like

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.100
Germany and Japan to help maintain peace, after an era of intense global war.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.280
"But we cannot expect these people to
discard Nazi ideas

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.290
with the casual air with which they
abandoned their uniform.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.490
Slowly, steadily. they must be taught the
fundamentals of democracy.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:27.000
Our task is to  supervise this training in
democracy."

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.160
Even though these countries are now stable, robust democracies,

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.810
and allies, US still has 50,000 troops in
Japan

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.760
and 54,000 in Germany.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.600
The Korean War brought an influx of American military

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.540
infrastructure to the Korean Peninsula,

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.040
where we now have 28,500 troops
stationed.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.520
The Cold War made the US expansion
to every corner of the globe

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.860
imperative to US strategic goals.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:58.000
"Here, in Russia, you see the reason why we are spending billions of dollars in military production

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.400
and why your family is paying the highest taxes in our history."

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.600
But even now that the Cold War's over

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.700
there's not a place on Earth that's not still
covered by US military influence.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.260
This includes everything from naval
compounds like

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.070
those in Guantanamo Bay, to small drone
bases like those in the Horn of Africa,

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:23.580
to little-known posts in the middle and
the Indian Ocean like that of Diego Garcia.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.160
And it's not cheap. Keeping the US
foreign bases

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:34.190
open, costs taxpayers between 70 and 100
billion dollars per year.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:37.530
That's more than any other government
agency receives

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.900
in taxpayer money, besides of course the
Department of Defense itself.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.480
Taxpayers pay between ten and forty thousand dollars more per soldier stationed abroad

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.620
than they do for those stationed here
domestically. But this cost seems to be

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:52.350
worth it to most lawmakers.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:56.990
The idea that US military leadership
provides an indispensable stability to the globe

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.360
is now a fixture of our global strategy
and our foreign policy.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.400
"In an uncertain world full of breathtaking
change,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.450
the one constant is American leadership."

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.620
"You can't have stability without American
leadership there's only one nation in

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:12.540
the world capable

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.890
of rallying the freedom-loving people
of this planet to confront evil."

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:20.740
"American leadership is not just
respected it is required."

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:23.700
So over the past seven years the
US has set up bases

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.380
all over the world in response to
threats and then they just never left.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:32.200
These eight hundred bases around the
world represent a massive system of

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:36.150
military power that isn't often talked
about, and it's us,

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.510
the American taxpayers, that are
financially supporting this.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:43.159
We've all decided that it's worth it to
pump a hundred billion dollars out of

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:47.100
our economy, to keep these bases open on
the far corners of our globe.

